e. 2015;526:531&#x2013;5.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26375009</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Scheller S, Yu H, Chadwick GL, McGlynn SE, Orphan VJ. Artificial electron acceptors decouple archaeal methane oxidation from sulfate reduction. Science. 2016;351:703&#x2013;7.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26912857</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Knittel K, Boetius A. Anaerobic oxidation of methane: progress with an unknown process. Annu Rev Microbiol. 2009;63:311&#x2013;34.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19575572</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wegener G, Krukenberg V, Riedel D, Tegetmeyer HE, Boetius A. Intercellular wiring enables electron transfer between methanotrophic archaea and bacteria. Nature. 2015;526:587&#x2013;90.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26490622</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Haroon MF, Hu S, Shi Y, Imelfort M, Keller J, Hugenholtz P, et al. Anaerobic oxidation of methane coupled to nitrate reduction in a novel archaeal lineage. Nature. 2013;500:567&#x2013;70.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">23892779</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Riedinger N, Formolo MJ, Lyons TW, Henkel S, Beck A, Kasten S. An inorganic geochemical argument for coupled anaerobic oxidation of methane and iron reduction in marine sediments. Geobiology. 2014;12:172&#x2013;81.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24460948</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Segarra KE, Comerford C, Slaughter J, Joye SB. Impact of electron acceptor availability on the anaerobic oxidation of methane in coastal freshwater and brackish wetland sediments. Geochimica Cosmochimica Acta. 2013;115:15&#x2013;30.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wankel SD, Adams MM, Johnston DT, Hansel CM, Joye SB, Girguis PR. Anaerobic m